u$ is the microwave frequency. EPR spectra are typically plotted as the first derivative of the absorption signal to enhance resolution and simplify data processing, allowing the g-factor to be calculated from the zero-crossing point of the first derivative. In solid-state EPR, the g-value is represented by three components, denoted as $\mathbf{g}_1$ , $\mathbf{g}_2$ , and $\mathbf{g}_3$ , or $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{x}}, \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{y}}$ , and $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{z}}$ . Depending on the symmetry of the electronic distribution, g-factor can be isotropic ( $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{x}} = \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{y}} = \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{z}} = \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{iso}}$ ), axial ( $\mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{x}} = \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{y}} = \mathbf{g}_{\perp} 
