eq \mathbf{g}_{\mathrm{z}}$ ) as shown in Figure 3f. The g-value of a free electron is 2.0023, and deviations from this value in the EPR spectra of cathode materials can indicate asymmetric electronic environments or the presence of specific oxygen species and oxidation states. Moreover, linewidth, defined as the full width at half maximum (FWHM) in the absorption spectrum or as the width between the maximum and minimum points in the first derivative spectrum, provides insight into relaxation processes, which is vital for understanding the dynamics of the electron spin system.[124] Unlike magnetic resonance spectroscopy, magnetometry measures changes in the bulk magnetization $(M)$ of a material in response to variations in $H$ or $T$ . Magnetic susceptibility $(\chi)$ is calculated by dividing the magnetization by the applied magnetic field. The $\chi - T$ curve of cathode materials provides critical information such as magnetic transition temperatures, Curie-Weiss temperature $(\Theta_{\mathrm{CW}})$ , Curie constant $(C)$ , and effective magnetic moment $(\mu_{\mathrm{eff}})$ . Both $\Theta_{\mathrm{CW}}$ and $C$ can be determined by fitting the $\chi - T$ curve to the Curie-Weiss law, $\chi = C / (T - \Theta_{\mathrm{CW}})$ , and $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ can then be calculated as $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}} = 8C\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ .[100] $\Theta_{\mathrm{CW}}$ is an indicator of the nature and strength of magnetic interaction, which is influenced by the number of unpaired electrons as well as the spatial arrangement of Li and TM ions. A negative $\Theta_{\mathrm{CW}}$ indicates predominantly antiferromagnetic interactions, a positive value indicates ferromagnetic interactions, and a zero value represents ideal paramagnetic behavior (Figure 3g). $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is generally used for comparative analyses with theoretical $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ values and indicates spin states of the paramagnetic atoms in the composition, which in turn indicates their oxidations states and electronic configurations. Additionally, field-cooled (FC) and zero field-cooled (ZFC) curves are often compared to analyzing magnetic properties. In the FC method, the sample is cooled with a magnetic field to align spins, while in the ZFC method, it is cooled without a field. In both cases, magnetization is measured during heating under an applied magnetic field. An important feature in this comparative analysis is the bifurcation of the two curves, which reflects the degree of magnetic ordering.[125] As solid-state NMR requires the MAS technique, powder samples must be loaded into a rotor and sealed with a cap. Accordingly, only as-synthesized powders or ex-situ electrode materials scraped from Al foil can be analyzed. EPR is primarily conducted on powder samples using tubes, while in-situ measurements require specially designed cells that minimize interference from EPR-inactive components. Similarly, magnetometry can be conducted both ex-situ and in-situ using systems such as the physical property measurement system (PPMS) or superconducting quantum interference device (SQUID). In-situ magnetometry requires specially designed cells to enable accurate measurements under operating conditions.

