eq})$ . The charge transfer resistance of the electrode reaction with a high activation energy is substantial, leading to an increase in polarization.[47] Electrodes with high loading possess an excess binder and tend to absorb too much electrolyte. The binder, being electronically insulating, combined with the infiltrated electrolyte, diminishes the electrode's electronic conductivity.[18,35,48] This reduced conductivity prolongs the time it takes for electron conduction to reach the reaction interface, leading to slower electron transfer kinetics.[48-50] As a result, electrodes with high loading are typically associated with various challenges, such as highly polarized, swift capacity deterioration, and poor rate capability.[36] Sun et al. employed $\mathrm{LiCoO}_2$ as the active material and used PVDF (10 wt%) as the binder to produce an ultrahigh-loading $(30\mathrm{mgcm}^{-2})$ flexible electrode.[36] This $\mathrm{LiCoO}_2@$ PVDF electrode demonstrated a low areal capacity of $1.55\mathrm{mAhcm}^{-2}$ and a rate capacity of $52\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $5\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . More concerning, the electrode lost its electrochemical activity at a high current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ due to pronounced polarization. Moreover, for electrodes utilizing electrically insulating active materials, such as the sulfur cathode, the electronic conductivity of high-loading electrodes can diminish even further, leading to a more pronounced degradation in electrochemical performance.[51] As mass loading rises, there is an inevitable increase in the electrode's thickness, such as a thickness reaching $460~\mu \mathrm{m}$ for the LFP/CNT/EVA70 (ethylene vinyl acetate with vinyl acetate contents of $70\mathrm{wt}\%$ ) electrode at $49\mathrm{mgcm}^{-2}$ . [50] This increased thickness results in a prolonged ion diffusion path.[49,50,52] Moreover, some ion channels in the high-loading electrode become obstructed, culminating in sluggish electrode reaction kinetics. The Marcus electron transfer theory postulates that the electron transfer reaction rate constant $(k_{\mathrm{et}})$ is influenced by the distance between the electron donor and acceptor, alterations in free energy, and the reorganization energy between the reactant and the encompassing solvent.[53] During the redox reaction on the electrode, the active materials either gain or lose electrons. In the context of the heterogeneous electrode, electrons function as donors. As per the Marcus electron transfer theory, the diminished ion diffusion coefficient in a high-loading electrode decreases the $k_{\mathrm{et}}$ . Consequently, this slow ion transport in the high-loading electrode hampers both the rate capability and cycling stability of the battery. As an illustration, our group developed a range of MCMB cathodes with varying areal densities using the traditional slurry-coating method. Observations indicated that as mass loading increased, conductivity fell sharply from $2.4 	imes 10^{6}$ to $1.1 	imes 10^{6} \mathrm{~S} \mathrm{~m}^{-1}$ ( $19 \mathrm{mg} \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ). Thus, the reversible capacity for the high-loading MCMB cathode ( $24 \mathrm{mg} \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) stands at only $21 \mathrm{mAh} \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $4 \mathrm{C}$ , a value which is a mere quarter of what is seen with the thinner cathode ( $99 \mathrm{mAh} \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $2 \mathrm{mg} \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ).[54] In conclusion, elevating the mass loading of active materials diminishes ionic conductivity, thereby undermining the battery's electrochemical performance.

